From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: adbtrr_work6_pr_fixes_use <>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 14:49:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] apps/examples: Add a test which exercises the heap quarantine

The existing mm_uaf_test shows the detector's reports, which have to be
read out of the log. The quarantine also has behaviour which can simply
be measured, so test it that way instead: each check prints pass or
fail and the exit status is non-zero if any failed.

  1. A freed address is not handed back by the next allocation of the
     same size. This is the whole point of holding chunks: it is what
     leaves a write through a stale pointer somewhere harmless to land.

  2. The delay is bounded. Churning one size many times must bring
     addresses back round, or the quarantine would just be a leak, and
     the number of addresses in circulation must stay near the ring
     depth. A model of the allocator settles at exactly one more than
     the configured depth, so the bound is set at twice the depth to
     leave room for coalescing on a real heap while still failing
     loudly on a leak.

  3. A chunk larger than the configured maximum is not held. Reported
     rather than asserted, because whether the identical address
     returns also depends on coalescing and on
     CONFIG_MM_ALLOC_LARGE_FROM_BACK.

  4. Held memory is released rather than failing an allocation. Fill
     the heap, empty it so the quarantine is holding as much as it may,
     then fill it again; the second pass must fit as much as the first.

Case 4 is the one worth having. Modelling the allocator with the flush
in mm_malloc() removed makes the second pass fall short by exactly the
ring depth, so this check goes red if that hook is ever dropped, and
that hook is the highest risk part of the feature.

The same model showed the check needs help to be meaningful: on a roomy
heap the fixed block cap is reached while memory is still available, so
the allocator never has to refuse and nothing is tested. The case now
reserves a ballast block first, and asserts that the allocator really
did run out.

Cases 5 and 6 run last and corrupt the heap deliberately, to show the
stale write after reuse and the double free of a held block. Those are
read from the log, since the report is the thing being demonstrated.
